ntdll: a query into a page behind the emulator's write trap runs again.

A system call fills the program's buffer from the Unix side. FEX keeps a read-write-execute page that holds
translated code read-execute (read-only for a DEP-promoted one) to see writes into that code: the program's own
write faults into FEX, which lets it through, but the Unix side's write has nobody to do that, so the call returns
STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION for a buffer the program can write. Windows never does. FEX's own note on NtReadFile names
the gap ("requires coordination between Wine and FEX").

Football Manager 2027 (Microsoft Store, Denuvo), M2 Pro, engine builds of 2026-10-20 and 2026-11-08: while a new
career loads, its protection calls NtQuerySystemInformation( SystemFirmwareTableInformation, 'RSMB' ) with a
0x5bd-byte buffer; get_firmware_info reads the header and faults on its first store (buffer + 0xc), the call
returns c0000005 and 12 ms later the thread dies in game_plugin.dll at an unmapped address, the same RVA on every
run.

When one of the query calls returns STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION and every page of its buffer is read-write for the
program (GuestProtection, 0043) while at least one is trapped, ntdll touches each page from the ARM64EC side with
an atomic OR of 0. That faults into FEX like a write of the program's (its handler takes native writes too),
which untraps the page and drops the translations, and the query runs once more. A buffer the program really
cannot write keeps its status and raises nothing, so a program that probes with read-only buffers sees what it saw
before. Covered: NtQuerySystemInformation, NtQuerySystemInformationEx, NtQueryInformationProcess,
NtQueryInformationThread, NtQueryObject, NtQueryVirtualMemory, all of which can run twice. An emulator without
GuestProtection changes nothing. WARN lines (seh channel) say when a query ran again and when it was left alone.

Not covered: calls that cannot simply run twice (NtReadFile, NtDeviceIoControlFile); those need the Unix side to
know the program's protection.

